\f0\fs24 \cf0 \\section\{Introduction\}\
\\label\{introduction\}\
\
\\textit\{Toxoplasma gondii\} is an obligate intracellular protozoan parasite capable of infecting nearly all warm-blooded animals, including humans and rodents. In intermediate hosts such as \\textit\{Mus musculus\} and \\textit\{Homo sapiens\}, infection occurs following ingestion of oocysts or tissue cysts and leads to toxoplasmosis, a disease that is often asymptomatic in immunocompetent individuals but can cause severe pathology in immunocompromised hosts or during pregnancy \\cite\{dubey_toxoplasma_1996\}. Due to its broad host range, well-characterized life cycle, and strong immunogenicity, \\textit\{T. gondii\} infection in mice represents a powerful experimental model for studying host\'96pathogen interactions and immune regulation.\
\
The lungs constitute a major site of immune activation during acute \\textit\{T. gondii\} infection. Following systemic dissemination, the parasite invades pulmonary tissue and replicates within macrophages and other immune cells, triggering inflammatory responses that impair lung function \\cite\{singhania_transcriptional_2019\}. Transcriptomic profiling has shown that pulmonary immune responses are dominated by interferon driven pathways, particularly those mediated by type I interferons (IFN-$\\alpha$/$\\beta$) and type II interferon (IFN-$\\gamma$), which are essential for controlling intracellular pathogens \\cite\{singhania_transcriptional_2019\}. These cytokines regulate the expression of interferon stimulated genes involved in antigen presentation, host defense, and immune cell recruitment.\
\
Importantly, immune responses to \\textit\{T. gondii\} are highly tissue specific. While some interferon inducible signatures are conserved between blood and lung, lung tissue exhibits a stronger and more complex transcriptional response characterized by pronounced interferon dependent gene expression and distinct inflammatory signatures \\cite\{singhania_transcriptional_2019\}. This highlights the importance of lung specific transcriptomic analyses to understand mechanisms of protection versus immunopathology.\
\
Type I and type II interferon signaling are mediated through their respective receptors, IFNAR and IFNGR, which are critical regulators of host defense during \\textit\{T. gondii\} infection. IFNAR signaling contributes to innate immune activation, while IFNGR signaling, activated by IFN-$\\gamma$, is essential for macrophage activation, antigen presentation, and control of intracellular parasite replication. Transcriptomic analyses have shown that IFN-$\\gamma$ signaling is a dominant driver of pulmonary immune responses and that many genes traditionally attributed to type I interferon signaling depend on IFNGR activity in this context \\cite\{singhania_transcriptional_2019\}. Genetic ablation of IFNAR and IFNGR therefore provides a framework to dissect interferon mediated transcriptional programs during infection.\
\
In this study, we perform differential gene expression analysis on lung RNA-seq samples from wild-type and interferon $\\alpha/\\gamma$ receptor double knockout mice under control and \\textit\{T. gondii\} infected conditions to characterize the impact of interferon receptor deficiency on pulmonary transcriptional responses.\
